#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087428 :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/de/deb7e20658882229a3653f4058c787bf2a5c4a26c5f2982406a2dba34c2d5846.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k(6^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2(6^{x})+4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725 :topic/math-academy-id 65 :topic/title "Solving One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513313 :question-generator/id #uuid "9fcb8d6f-e996-5bb7-a671-b20495799375" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186087824.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdb66394-4266-565e-91ae-8a8b7af9cee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 2$ and $c = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085101} {:answer/id #uuid "150b9369-075a-5021-bc4c-61c7a18b4f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 2$ and $c =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085102} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbf6db9-8223-5028-ae5e-f807b2dd3498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/