id "1c02b8e8-173d-552f-97ad-3130b712022c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=3$ and $c=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513248} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e89a2c6-e229-5c7a-a726-905b5cd23de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513244}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edd7a639-0c7a-5f31-bc22-4646381ff78d" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6dc0324ad749f7514e9cf683425a3591308bb4dd11bcbcf84b8361d3c4b365d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({3}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({3}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ so $c=-3.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({3}^{x})-3.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,0),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({3}^{x})-3 \\\\ 0 & =k({3}^{0})-3 \\\\ 0 & =k(1)-3 \\\\ 0 & =k-3 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=3$ and $c=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f27fce-4ec9-5f8a-88af-4fdd992da959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({2}^{x})-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513245} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1da0e2-51f0-5b4d-a576-6f6c0517dcd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.75({2}^{x})+0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513260} {:answer/id #uuid "66915a27-74fb-5185-82b5-c8aff15188be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513272} {:answer/id #uuid "6e293adf-9de3-5e23-88dd-4053bc7d4a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-0.75({2}^{x})+2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513273} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cbb0b0-935e-583f-ac78-32d85f13fd3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.75({2}^{x})-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0cbb0b0-935e-583f-ac78-32d85f13fd3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.75({2}^{x})-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513297} :answer-field/id #uuid "be6f6c1e-5eb5-557d-b1b5-dbe75a6368ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513301}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f205ec06-4d49-5209-9a3f-863c1b383f0c" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7d0622d73cf18a920b4c9716a53f885ff41979dd1cead73051bdd55c413676a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-0.75,$ so $c=-0.75.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-0.75.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ Reading the calibrated grid, the marked point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-0.75 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-0.75 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-0.75 \\\\ 2 & =k-0.75 \\\\ 2.75 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2.75({2}^{x})-0.75.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148a8a03-6ce9-595e-999e-f4d0bb2820f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=2$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513246} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbb827f-1fc8-5271-8dc7-57347760474d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=4$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513263} {:answer/id #uuid "783f9229-d404-52df-878e-dde782c8cef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=6$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513276} {:answer/id #uuid "83b8994e-1980-578f-bf73-981e654d0977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=6$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513280} {:answer/id #uuid "8b98c4b3-aec9-5dd9-9654-ae8ea7850750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=2$ and $c=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "148a8a03-6ce9-595e-999e-f4d0bb2820f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=2$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513246} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42f164a-8f93-5002-9420-b8b8cc5382ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513304}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f40f3edb-b8d0-568f-8130-1e38166ca261" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0afc2271629eaae38b1c88324f462c15b995e1547aa1128bf0781271b722cd0f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({4}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({4}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({4}^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({4}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({4}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=2$ and $c=4.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}]}
